# @PROJECT_NAME@

Generated Qornix Web application.

This is the default application template created by:

```bash
./create_new_project.sh ../@PROJECT_NAME@
```

It is a clean C++ web application scaffold with:

- landing page at `/`
- JSON health endpoint at `/health`
- documentation pages at `/docs`
- route and handler examples
- async HTTP route examples at `/async/ping` and `/async/sleep/{ms}`
- static asset serving from `/static/{filename}`
- portable deploy bundle under `build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@`

For Schema Manager, Dynamic API, Query Builder and Table Browser, generate a dynamic API app instead:

```bash
./create_new_project.sh ../my_api --with-dynamic-api
```

For the same Dynamic API backend plus a Vue/Vite frontend scaffold, generate:

```bash
./create_new_project.sh ../my_vue --with-dynamic-api-vue
```

## Build

```bash
mkdir -p build
cd build
cmake ..
cmake --build .
```

## Run from deploy bundle

```bash
cd build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@
./@PROJECT_NAME@
```

Then open:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8008/
http://127.0.0.1:8008/health
http://127.0.0.1:8008/docs
http://127.0.0.1:8008/async/ping
http://127.0.0.1:8008/async/sleep/10
```

## Development run

You can also run the raw executable from the build directory:

```bash
cd build
./@PROJECT_NAME@
```

The application can find resources from the source tree during development and from the deploy bundle in production.

## Project structure

```text
@PROJECT_NAME@/
├── CMakeLists.txt
├── README.md
├── config.yaml
├── main.cpp
├── routes.h
├── app_paths.h
├── handlers/
├── templates/
├── static/
├── doc/
├── route_extensions/
└── logs/
```

## Add your first route

Create a handler under `handlers/`, include it in `routes.h`, and register it:

```cpp
server.add_route("/hello", std::make_shared<HelloHandler>());
```

See:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8008/docs/routing
```

## Deploy to another directory or machine

Copy the full deploy bundle:

```bash
cp -R build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@ /opt/@PROJECT_NAME@
cd /opt/@PROJECT_NAME@
./@PROJECT_NAME@
```

Or run with an explicit application root:

```bash
QORNIX_APP_ROOT=/opt/@PROJECT_NAME@ /opt/@PROJECT_NAME@/@PROJECT_NAME@
```

The bundle includes app assets but does not include system shared libraries. Check them with:

```bash
ldd ./@PROJECT_NAME@
```

## Runtime Docker image

The template includes `runtime-Dockerfile`, which builds a runtime image around the deploy bundle. Build the app first:

```bash
mkdir -p build
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build --target @PROJECT_NAME@_deploy
docker build -f runtime-Dockerfile -t @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime .
```

Run with the config embedded in the image:

```bash
docker run --rm -p 8008:8008 @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime
```

To override config or persist file logs:

```bash
mkdir -p docker/config logs
cp build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@/config.yaml docker/config/config.yaml
```

Set file logging in `docker/config/config.yaml` when needed:

```yaml
server:
  address: 0.0.0.0
  port: 8008
logging:
  enabled: true
  to_file: true
  file_path: /logs/server
```

Run with mounted config and logs:

```bash
sudo chown -R 10001:10001 logs
docker run -d --name @PROJECT_NAME@ \
  -p 8008:8008 \
  -v "$PWD/docker/config/config.yaml:/app/config.yaml:ro" \
  -v "$PWD/logs:/logs" \
  @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime
```

To move the app to another computer without a registry:

```bash
docker save @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime -o @PROJECT_NAME@-runtime.tar
```

Copy `@PROJECT_NAME@-runtime.tar` plus any external config/log directories you want to keep. On the target machine, run `docker load -i @PROJECT_NAME@-runtime.tar` and start the container with the same `docker run` command.

The Dockerfile copies `build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@/` into `/app`, sets `QORNIX_APP_ROOT=/app`, makes the binary executable and changes the container config to listen on `0.0.0.0` so published ports work. It uses Ubuntu 24.04 runtime package names; if you change enabled modules or database drivers, inspect `ldd build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@/@PROJECT_NAME@` and adjust the package list.

## Enable ORM

ORM is disabled by default in generated applications to keep the first build minimal.
Enable it when needed:

```bash
cmake -D@PROJECT_NAME_UPPER@_ENABLE_ORM=ON ..
```

## Framework path

The generated `CMakeLists.txt` points to the Qornix Web checkout used by the generator.
Override it if the framework moves:

```bash
cmake -DQORNIX_WEB_ROOT=/path/to/qornix_web ..
```


## Async routes

The default template includes coroutine route examples registered from `routes.h` and implemented in `handlers/async_handlers.h`:

```cpp
server.get_async("/async/ping", makeAsyncPingHandler());
server.get_async("/async/sleep/{ms}", makeAsyncSleepHandler());
```

Use async handlers for timers, database calls, network calls and any operation that would otherwise block an `io_context` worker thread. Keep sync handlers for cheap CPU-only or static responses.


## Dynamic API frontend templates

For the same Dynamic API backend with a frontend scaffold, use either React or Angular:

```bash
./create_new_project.sh ../my_react --with-dynamic-api-react
./create_new_project.sh ../my_angular --with-dynamic-api-angular
```

See `dynamic_api_react_app_template.md` for React and `dynamic_api_angular_app_template.md` for Angular.
